  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nExtended range model data suggests that a developing surface low \nwill track eastward across the northern Mid-Atlantic states on \nTuesday night/Wednesday, in conjunction with an amplifying longwave \ntrough across the eastern CONUS. A cold front attached to the low \nappears likely to drop southward through the TN Valley during this \ntimeframe, although details regarding specific timing are unclear at \nthis point. A warm/moist airmass is expected to be in place ahead of \nthe front, with steep low/mid-level lapse rates and CAPE to support a\ncouple of rounds of thunderstorms (perhaps an MCS Tuesday night \nfollowed by additional convection more directly forced by the cold \nfront Wednesday). Furthermore, deep-layer NW flow of 20-30 knots will\nexist within a contracted height gradient between the trough to our \neast and a strong mid-level high over the southwestern CONUS, and \nthis could support a risk for damaging winds given the favorable \nthermodynamic environment.\n\nIn the wake of the front, light-moderate northerly winds will advect\na cooler/drier continental airmass into the region, with dewpoints \nbriefly falling into the 60s an PWAT values falling to around or \nslightly below 1\" from Wednesday night-Thursday night (and perhaps \ninto Friday as well). By the end of the forecast period, deep-layer \nSSW flow will return to the region as an amplifying shortwave trough \n(traveling around the periphery of the ridge to our west) digs \nsoutheastward into the Mid-MS/Lower-OH Valley region and a surface \nlow shifts eastward from the Red River Valley into the Arklamiss \nvicinity. Rich moisture will return to the region as this occurs, \nwith an increase in the coverage of showers/thunderstorms expected \nbeginning late Friday night and continuing through Saturday.",
